I’m checking the exact CSS relationship so I can answer the simplest correct version and avoid a brittle override.## Why not a transform on `.is-home`?

Because `transform` affects the whole element, not just the background image.

On [docs/.vitepress/theme/pantoken.css](docs/.vitepress/theme/pantoken.css), `.is-home` is the actual page container. If we did:

```css
html[dir="rtl"] .is-home {
  transform: scaleX(-1);
}
```

that would flip the whole home surface, including text and any content inside it — not just the decorative art in the background.

## Better pattern

The clean option is to keep the background image on a separate layer, such as a pseudo-element:

```css
.is-home::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background-image: var(--vp-home-bg-image);
  background-repeat: no-repeat;
  background-size: cover;
  background-position: right bottom;
  pointer-events: none;
}

html[dir="rtl"] .is-home::before {
  transform: scaleX(-1);
}
```

That mirrors only the artwork, while the actual home content stays normal.

> In other words: a background override is the right fit here, while a transform on the same element would be too broad.

